<DocsLayout
  title="Session brief and peer radar — Kvendra developer docs"
  description="How an agent resumes where you left off with whoami's session brief, sees other writers around the same entities with the peer radar, and pulls changes with check_notifications."
>
    <span class="kvd-kicker">Reference</span>
    <h1>Session brief and peer radar</h1>
    <p>
      Every new agent session starts without memory of the last one. Instead of
      re-explaining where you were, the agent can ask the knowledge base. The
      answer is <strong>derived from facts the engine already keeps</strong> —
      your transactions and the server-side entity history — not from a
      separate log that could drift out of date.
    </p>
    <p class="kvd-callout">
      <strong>Hosted only.</strong> The session brief, entity reservations and
      <code>check_notifications</code> are part of the hosted service. Liveness and the peer radar need a multi-actor workspace (Team
      or Enterprise). A self-hosted Platform ignores <code>include_brief</code>
      and answers a plain <code>whoami</code>, so skills that ask for a brief
      keep working there — they just get none.
    </p>

    <h2>The session brief</h2>
    <pre><code>whoami({'{'} include_brief: true, max_events: 20, since: "2026-10-01T00:00:00Z" {'}'})</code></pre>
    <p>
      Without <code>include_brief</code> the response is the usual identity.
      With it, the response gains a <code>brief</code> object for
      <strong>you</strong>, the caller:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>open_txns</code> — your transactions still in progress, with their
        type, trigger, start time and recovery notes: the "where did I stop"
        cursor;
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>recent_activity</code> — your latest entity edits (entity id,
        version before and after, change summary, trigger, date), capped by
        <code>max_events</code> and bounded by <code>since</code>, with a
        <code>truncated</code> flag when there is more.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The brief is pull-only and read-only: asking for it never changes
      anything. The Skills read it at session start when it is present.
    </p>

    <h2>Liveness and the peer radar (Team and Enterprise)</h2>
    <p>
      In a multi-actor workspace the brief also carries your own
      <strong>liveness</strong> — a "last seen" indicator derived from your most
      recent activity (active, idle, or older), not an online/offline signal.
      Add <code>radar: true</code> for the <strong>peer radar</strong>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>whoami({'{'} include_brief: true, radar: true {'}'})
whoami({'{'} include_brief: true, radar: true, radar_all_actors: true {'}'})   // owner / admin</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>
        By default the radar covers your <strong>neighbourhood</strong>: other
        writer-units with in-progress transactions on, or recent edits to, the
        entities you touched and their direct neighbours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>radar_all_actors: true</code> spans the whole workspace. It takes
        effect only for roles allowed to see every in-progress transaction
        (owner, admin); for anyone else it silently falls back to the
        neighbourhood instead of failing.
      </li>
      <li>
        The radar never crosses workspaces, and descriptions of what a peer is
        doing are computed on demand from the facts, never stored.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Visibility follows roles</h2>
    <p>
      The brief and the radar show only what your role may see. You always see
      your own transactions. Roles without workspace-wide transaction
      visibility — a viewer, for example — do not see other actors'
      in-progress transactions or their drafts: the radar gives such a caller
      the <em>count</em> of nearby peer transactions, not their detail.
    </p>

    <h2>Pulling changes: <code>check_notifications</code></h2>
    <p>
      When you read an entity you intend to change, you can reserve it:
      <code>entity_get</code> with <code>intent: "soft-announce"</code> (a
      cooperative, non-blocking announcement) or <code>intent: "hard-lock"</code>
      (exclusive: other writers get <code>409 LOCKED</code> until it is
      released). Both need an <code>agent_instance_id</code>.
    </p>
    <pre><code>check_notifications({'{'} agent_instance_id: "&lt;your-agent-instance&gt;", max_events: 50 {'}'})</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>check_notifications</code> then returns what <em>other</em>
      writer-units changed on the entities you reserved since your last check
      (your own changes are excluded), plus the open notices in your
      <strong>dispute inbox</strong>. There is no push: the transport is
      pull-only, so call it at session start and before you write. It is
      read-only and never blocks a write — the hard guarantee against lost
      updates is still the <code>expected_version</code> check described in
      <a href="/docs/pipelines/#concurrent-writers">Concurrent writers</a>.
    </p>
